       "content": "# Scope and Limitations\n\n## Scope of the Study\n\nThis research is designed to investigate and define the effectiveness of a specialized drilling tool\u2014equipped with integrated alignment guides\u2014in promoting accurate and consistent drilling centers across multiple repeated trials. The study is bounded by the following parameters:\n\n**1. Primary Objective and Focus**\nThe central aim of this research is to evaluate the tool's capacity to produce repeatable, centered holes under controlled experimental conditions. The investigation will quantify the degree of positional accuracy (radial and axial deviation) and consistency (standard deviation and coefficient of variation) achieved across a defined number of repeated drilling trials, establishing a statistical baseline for the tool's performance.\n\n**2. Alignment Guide Functionality**\nA dedicated component of the study examines the mechanical and geometric functionality of the tool's alignment guides. This includes assessing how the guides interact with the workpiece surface, the degree of self-centering they provide, their resistance to lateral deflection under cutting forces, and their contribution to reducing operator-dependent variability. The study will characterize the guides' role in constraining the drill bit's entry angle and maintaining a perpendicular or specified-angle trajectory.\n\n**3. Workpiece Dimensions**\nThe tool's performance will be assessed across a defined range of workpiece dimensions, including variations in:\n- Diameter and thickness of cylindrical workpieces\n- Length and cross-sectional area of flat and prismatic stock\n- Edge proximity (holes drilled near the boundary of the workpiece)\n\nThe dimensional range is selected to represent common industrial and workshop applications while remaining within the tool's designed operational envelope.\n\n**4. Material Range**\nThe study will evaluate the tool's precision-maint",
